<p>&#8220;How important is it for a typical university to maintain a department in each of the following fields?&#8221;<span id="more-58190"></span></p>
<p>A survey by <a href="https://polarizationresearchlab.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Polarization Research Lab</a> asked people that question. Fifteen fields were listed, and respondents were given four options:</p>
<ul>
<li>Essential &#8212; a university should always have this department</li>
<li>Important, but could be cut if necessary</li>
<li>Not very important</li>
<li>This department should be cut.</li>
</ul>
<p>The field with the most public support, according to this survey, is English, with 86% of the respondents calling it &#8220;Essential&#8221; and another 11% calling it &#8220;Important&#8221;. In spots 2 and 3 were Mathematics and History. Very few deemed these fields &#8220;not very important&#8221; or said they &#8220;should be cut&#8221;.</p>
<p>At the bottom of the list was Gender and Sexuality Studies, with 24% of the responses saying it is &#8220;Essential,&#8221; 28% saying it is &#8220;Important&#8221;, 21% saying &#8220;not very important,&#8221; and 28% saying &#8220;should be cut.&#8221;</p>
<p>Where does philosophy fall on the list? Close to the bottom, with 34% calling it &#8220;Essential,&#8221; 45% calling it &#8220;Important&#8221;, 17% saying it is &#8220;not very important&#8221;, and 4% saying it should be cut.</p>
